WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.732
Elimination solves A x equals b in

2
00:00:05.815 --> 00:00:08.397
about n cubed over three steps.

3
00:00:08.397 --> 00:00:10.480
With a million unknowns

4
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.312
that is out of reach

5
00:00:12.396 --> 00:00:14.894
even though almost every entry

6
00:00:14.978 --> 00:00:17.060
of such a matrix is zero.

7
00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.030
So we do something different

8
00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.837
start with a guess

9
00:00:21.837 --> 00:00:24.729
and improve it again and again

10
00:00:24.729 --> 00:00:27.530
using only products A times x.

11
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.272
If the system has a solution

12
00:00:30.272 --> 00:00:33.158
will such a method always find it?

13
00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:34.560
Pause and guess.

14
00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.582
A small system: four x plus y equals five

15
00:00:41.582 --> 00:00:44.205
and x plus three y equals four.

16
00:00:44.205 --> 00:00:46.910
Each line is drawn in the plane

17
00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.860
they cross at the answer, one, one.

18
00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.356
Jacobi solves each equation

19
00:00:52.435 --> 00:00:54.025
for its own unknown

20
00:00:54.025 --> 00:00:56.727
using the old value of the other.

21
00:00:56.727 --> 00:00:59.191
Only the diagonal is inverted

22
00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:01.098
one division per entry.

23
00:01:01.098 --> 00:01:02.290
Start at zero.

24
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.404
The first step lands at one point two five

25
00:01:06.404 --> 00:01:08.417
one point three three.

26
00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:11.305
The next at zero point nine two

27
00:01:11.305 --> 00:01:13.144
zero point nine two.

28
00:01:13.144 --> 00:01:17.082
Then closer, and closer, circling in on one

29
00:01:17.082 --> 00:01:17.582
one.

30
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.682
Why does it work?

31
00:01:19.775 --> 00:01:22.195
The exact answer satisfies

32
00:01:22.288 --> 00:01:23.963
the same equation

33
00:01:23.963 --> 00:01:28.058
so the error obeys e next equals M times e

34
00:01:28.058 --> 00:01:30.663
with an iteration matrix M.

35
00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:33.641
After k steps the error is M to

36
00:01:33.734 --> 00:01:36.340
the k times the first error.

37
00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:40.633
That goes to zero exactly when every eigenvalue

38
00:01:40.717 --> 00:01:43.486
of M is smaller than one in size.

39
00:01:43.486 --> 00:01:47.093
The largest size, the spectral radius rho

40
00:01:47.093 --> 00:01:50.869
is the factor the error shrinks by per step

41
00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:53.050
here zero point two nine.

42
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.869
Gauss Seidel uses each new value

43
00:01:55.946 --> 00:01:57.566
as soon as it has it.

44
00:01:57.566 --> 00:01:59.649
Its path goes straight in

45
00:01:59.726 --> 00:02:02.117
and its spectral radius is zero

46
00:02:02.194 --> 00:02:05.357
point zero eight three, Jacobi's squared.

47
00:02:05.357 --> 00:02:08.674
On the three by three system in the notes

48
00:02:08.674 --> 00:02:10.063
the same happens

49
00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:12.840
twenty four steps against thirteen.

50
00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.479
So does every solvable system work?

51
00:02:16.479 --> 00:02:18.227
Take this matrix B.

52
00:02:18.227 --> 00:02:21.024
Its determinant is minus three

53
00:02:21.024 --> 00:02:24.783
so B is invertible, and the answer is one

54
00:02:24.783 --> 00:02:25.283
one.

55
00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:27.803
Run Jacobi from zero.

56
00:02:27.803 --> 00:02:29.291
Three, three.

57
00:02:29.291 --> 00:02:32.587
Then minus three, minus three.

58
00:02:32.587 --> 00:02:36.095
Then nine, nine, off the screen

59
00:02:36.095 --> 00:02:40.242
then minus fifteen, then thirty three.

60
00:02:40.242 --> 00:02:41.730
It runs away.

61
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.821
Its iteration matrix has eigenvalues

62
00:02:45.925 --> 00:02:49.458
plus and minus two, so rho is two.

63
00:02:49.458 --> 00:02:52.575
B is not diagonally dominant

64
00:02:52.679 --> 00:02:55.589
each diagonal one is smaller

65
00:02:55.693 --> 00:02:58.083
than the two beside it.

66
00:02:58.083 --> 00:03:01.200
Being solvable is not enough.

67
00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.135
Now a real size.

68
00:03:03.135 --> 00:03:05.931
A square plate is heated evenly

69
00:03:05.931 --> 00:03:08.388
with its edges held at zero.

70
00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:10.760
On a twenty by twenty grid

71
00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.386
that is four hundred unknowns

72
00:03:13.471 --> 00:03:16.013
and only one point two percent

73
00:03:16.097 --> 00:03:18.300
of the matrix is not zero.

74
00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.033
Jacobi converges

75
00:03:20.115 --> 00:03:22.475
but it needs one thousand two

76
00:03:22.556 --> 00:03:24.753
hundred and sixteen sweeps.

77
00:03:24.753 --> 00:03:28.090
Gauss Seidel needs six hundred and nine.

78
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.227
Rho is about zero point nine eight nine

79
00:03:32.321 --> 00:03:34.783
so each sweep removes only

80
00:03:34.877 --> 00:03:37.244
one percent of the error.

81
00:03:37.339 --> 00:03:39.800
Converging is not the same

82
00:03:39.895 --> 00:03:41.693
as converging fast.

83
00:03:41.693 --> 00:03:45.480
The next method needs thirty two steps.

84
00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.323
For a symmetric positive definite matrix

85
00:03:49.402 --> 00:03:52.665
solving the system is the same as finding

86
00:03:52.745 --> 00:03:55.052
the bottom of an energy bowl.

87
00:03:55.052 --> 00:03:56.803
Here is a small bowl

88
00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:59.031
drawn by its contour lines

89
00:03:59.031 --> 00:04:01.180
the bottom is at one, one.

90
00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.842
Steepest descent always walks

91
00:04:03.922 --> 00:04:05.357
straight downhill.

92
00:04:05.357 --> 00:04:08.148
In a long, narrow bowl it zigzags

93
00:04:08.148 --> 00:04:11.258
each step partly undoing the last one

94
00:04:11.258 --> 00:04:13.730
and creeps towards the bottom.

95
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.909
Conjugate gradients start downhill too

96
00:04:16.982 --> 00:04:20.102
but then correct each new direction so that

97
00:04:20.174 --> 00:04:23.004
it is K orthogonal to the earlier ones.

98
00:04:23.076 --> 00:04:25.543
A step along a new direction never

99
00:04:25.616 --> 00:04:27.357
spoils the old progress.

100
00:04:27.357 --> 00:04:28.735
In two dimensions

101
00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:32.000
the second step lands exactly at the bottom.

102
00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:33.279
On the plate

103
00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.709
CG needs thirty two steps instead

104
00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:37.066
of twelve hundred.

105
00:04:37.066 --> 00:04:40.282
Its pace is set by the square root of kappa

106
00:04:40.282 --> 00:04:42.712
about thirteen, not kappa itself

107
00:04:42.712 --> 00:04:45.070
about one hundred seventy eight.

108
00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:48.651
So: an iteration multiplies the error

109
00:04:48.732 --> 00:04:51.166
by the same matrix every step

110
00:04:51.166 --> 00:04:54.248
and its speed is the spectral radius.

111
00:04:54.248 --> 00:04:56.843
Conjugate gradients go further

112
00:04:56.843 --> 00:05:00.250
because they never undo earlier progress.

113
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:01.510
Next part

114
00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:05.241
the same idea of improving step by step

115
00:05:05.241 --> 00:05:07.880
used to compute eigenvalues.
